1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,125
Dune's number is minus 0.58:
the highest of any clinic.

2
00:00:04,165 --> 00:00:08,685
On that number, Dune is the
worst performer in the cast.

3
00:00:08,725 --> 00:00:10,803
It comes from 12 patients.

4
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:16,440
Alder is just behind, at
minus 0.65, from 30 patients.

5
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:21,054
If you had to bet on which clinic is truly
the worst, would you still pick Dune?

6
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,514
Many analysts would not. They would
pull every estimate toward the average,

7
00:00:25,554 --> 00:00:28,563
and pull hardest where
there is least to go on.

8
00:00:28,603 --> 00:00:30,527
That is the random-effect model,

9
00:00:30,567 --> 00:00:34,414
and this episode is about what
it buys and what it costs.

10
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,221
Welcome back to Model in Motion.

11
00:00:38,261 --> 00:00:40,297
Today: fixed or random?

12
00:00:42,367 --> 00:00:45,406
So far, each provider has
had a number of its own,

13
00:00:45,446 --> 00:00:50,284
and we have assumed nothing about those
numbers. That is what fixed means.

14
00:00:50,324 --> 00:00:52,333
The alternative assumes something:

15
00:00:52,373 --> 00:00:57,245
that the numbers are draws from one
bell curve, with a centre and a spread.

16
00:00:57,367 --> 00:01:02,626
Then each estimate is a compromise between
the provider's own data and the centre.

17
00:01:02,666 --> 00:01:06,372
The weight on its own data
is called its reliability.

18
00:01:06,412 --> 00:01:12,590
For Dune, with a spread of 0.33 borrowed
from our crowd, the reliability is 0.20.

19
00:01:12,630 --> 00:01:16,896
Dune keeps a fifth of its
distance from the centre.

20
00:01:17,033 --> 00:01:21,228
Do that for every clinic.
Dune moves to minus 1.33.

21
00:01:21,268 --> 00:01:27,276
Alder, with more patients, keeps
two fifths, and stops at minus 1.18.

22
00:01:27,316 --> 00:01:32,231
The order has flipped. And Elm, which
had no estimate at all, now has one.

23
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,825
Now the crowd of two
hundred, fitted both ways.

24
00:01:37,865 --> 00:01:43,101
Each line joins a provider's fixed-effect
estimate to its random-effect one.

25
00:01:43,141 --> 00:01:47,705
Small providers move most. Under 50 patients,

26
00:01:47,745 --> 00:01:53,518
a typical reliability is
0.37. Above 200, it is 0.83.

27
00:01:53,667 --> 00:02:00,091
The estimates draw together. Their
spread falls from 0.44 to 0.26.

28
00:02:00,131 --> 00:02:01,858
The true spread is 0.33.

29
00:02:01,898 --> 00:02:07,329
Fixed effects scatter wider than
the truth; random effects, narrower.

30
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:12,513
What does the pull buy? Accuracy, on average.

31
00:02:12,553 --> 00:02:16,900
We know the truth in this crowd, so
we can measure the typical error.

32
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:26,741
It falls from 0.28 to 0.20. For providers
under 50 patients, from 0.40 to 0.25.

33
00:02:29,333 --> 00:02:33,944
Now the costs. First, the pull
does not know who truly differs.

34
00:02:33,984 --> 00:02:37,954
Our six planted providers
sit 0.8 from the centre.

35
00:02:37,994 --> 00:02:44,973
Fixed effects put them at 0.67, on
average. Random effects, at 0.46.

36
00:02:45,013 --> 00:02:49,224
And fixed effects flag four of
the six; random effects, three.

37
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:53,516
The second cost is subtler. In this crowd,

38
00:02:53,556 --> 00:02:56,928
providers with sicker
patients truly perform better.

39
00:02:56,968 --> 00:03:00,471
The random-effect model
assumes there is no such link,

40
00:03:00,511 --> 00:03:03,289
and pulls everyone toward one centre.

41
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:06,575
So its errors follow the case mix.

42
00:03:06,615 --> 00:03:12,417
Their correlation with it is
0.35. For fixed effects, 0.06.

43
00:03:12,457 --> 00:03:16,924
The sickest fifth of providers
look 0.10 worse than they are.

44
00:03:16,964 --> 00:03:19,826
The healthiest fifth, 0.10 better.

45
00:03:19,967 --> 00:03:23,713
Break the link, so that performance
and case mix are unrelated,

46
00:03:23,753 --> 00:03:25,906
and that correlation vanishes.

47
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:30,599
So which? If you are judging
individual providers,

48
00:03:30,639 --> 00:03:34,372
and performance may be tied
to case mix, fixed effects.

49
00:03:34,412 --> 00:03:36,522
If you have many tiny providers,

50
00:03:36,562 --> 00:03:41,482
or your question is about providers
as a population, random effects.

51
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:46,189
Either way, the estimates feed the
same standardization from Episode 11.

52
00:03:46,229 --> 00:03:51,331
The choice changes the numbers that go
in, not the arithmetic that follows.

53
00:03:51,371 --> 00:03:55,774
And neither one protects you from
a risk factor that nobody recorded.

54
00:03:57,367 --> 00:03:58,863
Let's gather it up.

55
00:03:58,903 --> 00:04:03,834
Random effects pull each estimate
toward the centre, by its reliability.

56
00:04:03,874 --> 00:04:08,634
The pull buys accuracy on average,
and gives every provider an estimate.

57
00:04:08,674 --> 00:04:12,309
It costs you the outliers, and
it bends with the case mix.

58
00:04:12,433 --> 00:04:16,361
Fixed effects have a cost of
their own that we have not faced.

59
00:04:16,401 --> 00:04:20,137
One intercept per provider
is a great many intercepts.

60
00:04:20,177 --> 00:04:22,596
Next time: too many intercepts.

61
00:04:22,733 --> 00:04:26,838
This has been Model in Motion.
Thanks for following the story.
